sec.p:
We model vϕ(x) as a two-layer linear network with parameters ϕ = (θ, w):
(2)
sd(x)=∑nθndxn,vϕ(x)=∑dwdsd(x), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{d}(\mathbf{x})=\sum_{n} \theta_{n d} x_{n}, \quad v_{\phi}(\mathbf{x})=\sum_{d} w_{d} s_{d}(\mathbf{x}), \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where n indexes sensory features and d indexes state representation features. For completeness and clarity, the matrix form is
(3)
vϕ(x)=wθ∣x. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} v_{\phi}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{w} \theta^{\mid} \mathbf{x} . \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

To make predictions about representation learning in OTu, we need to understand how the updates in Eq. 4 should manifest in OTu activity changes. Plugging the update into Eq. 2 and applying the one-hot assumption for x yields:
(5)
Δsd(x)∝−δwd. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \Delta s_{d}(\mathbf{x}) \propto-\delta w_{d} . \tag{S} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The weight update in Eq. 4 is a linear dynamical system. We treat the weights as latent variables and RPE δ and reward r as observed variables at each time step, as shown in Figure 1B. If we assume additive Gaussian noise in the weight updates and observations, then this is a linear-Gaussian system. Specifically,
(6)
w(t+1)=w(t)+β(t)δ(t)s+ξ(t) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbf{w}(t+1)=\mathbf{w}(t)+\beta(t) \delta(t) \mathbf{s}+\xi(t) \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(7)
ξ(t)∼i.i.d.N(0,σupdate2ID) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \xi(t) \stackrel{\text { i.i.d. }}{\sim} \mathcal{N}\left(0, \sigma_{\text {update }}^{2} \mathbf{I}_{D}\right) \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(8)
w(1)∼N(0,σinit2ID) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbf{w}(1) \sim \mathcal{N}\left(0, \sigma_{\mathrm{init}}^{2} \mathbf{I}_{D}\right) \tag{8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(9)
δ(t)=r(t)−w(t)⋅s(t)+η(t) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \delta(t)=r(t)-\mathbf{w}(t) \cdot \mathbf{s}(t)+\eta(t) \tag{9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(10)
η(t)∼i.i.d.N(0,σobs2) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \eta(t) \stackrel{\text { i.i.d. }}{\sim} \mathcal{N}\left(0, \sigma_{o b s}^{2}\right) \tag{10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where the noise terms, ξ(t) and η(t), are sampled independently at each step t = 1, …, T − 1. We make the simplifying assumption that the noise is isotropic and the initial weights are multivariate Gaussian. We make an additional assumption that the subjective value of the water reward is constant throughout the session even as the mouse reaches satiety. Lastly, we parameterize the learning rate as β(t)=βinit(1+toffset)t+toffset\begin{document}$\beta(t)=\frac{\beta_{\text {init }}\left(1+t_{\text {offset }}\right)}{t+t_{\text {offset }}}$\end{document}, which permits delaying the learning rate decay through toffset and scales the learning rate through βinit. In the case where learning rate is held constant, we simply set and infer β(t) = βinit.
